Actually, the phrasing "Two kagamimochi are distinguished if at least one of the mochi is different" implies that if we have two mochi of the same size, they are distinct entities. So if we have two mochi of size 4, say at indices $i$ and $k$, and one of size 2 at index $j$, then $(A_j, A_i)$ and $(A_j, A_k)$ are two different kagamimochi.
However, usually in these types of problems, if the sizes are the same, the condition $a \le b/2$ is the same.
